# Disable autocrlf on generated files, they always generate with LF
# Add any extra files or paths here to make git stop saying they
# are changed when only line endings change.
src/generated/**/.cache/* text eol=lf
src/generated/**/*.json text eol=lf

# ==========================================================================================
# Line endings
# ==========================================================================================
# Sources, data and the tools are pinned to LF, because LF is what must end up inside the built jar.
#
# This is not cosmetic. The generators assemble their JSON out of here-strings, and a here-string inherits the line
# endings of the script that contains it. While these scripts were pinned to CRLF, every generated resource had CRLF
# in the working tree; git normalised on add so the committed bytes were LF either way, but Gradle copies the
# WORKING TREE bytes into the jar. A Windows build therefore produced a jar whose 521 JSON entries each differed
# from the released one by one byte per line - which silently broke the only check this repository has for
# "did the build change?": comparing the jar fingerprint against the release. It reported a difference every time,
# and so told us nothing.
#
# PowerShell 5.1 parses an LF-only script without complaint. What it does NOT tolerate is a non-ASCII .ps1 written
# without a BOM, because it then decodes it with the system code page - that is why the tools are pure ASCII, and
# that rule is unrelated to line endings.
#
# cmd.exe and bat/cmd wrappers still want CRLF, so they keep it.
*.java       text eol=lf
*.json       text eol=lf
*.tsv        text eol=lf
*.gradle     text eol=lf
*.properties text eol=lf
*.md         text eol=lf
*.yml        text eol=lf
*.ps1        text eol=lf
*.cmd        text eol=crlf
*.bat        text eol=crlf

# Binary formats: git must never touch these
*.png binary
*.jar binary
